    "review": "THIS IS PERFECTLY FINE\n\nThe analyzed binary corresponds to a standard Google Chrome installer. Strongest raw evidence indicates a benign file: the file integrity check passed with a valid digital signature matching Google LLC issued by DigiCert. PE section metrics demonstrate normal entropy across all sections without indicators of commercial packing, compression, or section anomalies. Furthermore, the file overlay consists entirely of the 18,872-byte digital certificate, confirming no trailing hidden payloads.\n\nImported functions and referenced strings are entirely consistent with legitimate installer and updater behavior. The imports include typical Windows API functions for process creation, token handling, service configuration, and WinHTTP networking required by Google Update. The extracted strings reference authentic Google infrastructure endpoints, such as dl.google.com, update.googleapis.com, and Crashpad telemetry handlers. There are no suspicious instruction patterns or malicious shell execution triggers present in the disassembled entry point.\n\nWeak indicators such as generic registry run keys and cookie strings reflect standard persistence mechanisms and web-related data handling intrinsic to browser installers. Limitations of this static analysis include unverified certificate chain revocation status, lack of external hash reputation data, unconfigured YARA rules, and absence of runtime execution monitoring.\n\nAppropriate next steps include validating the certificate chain against the operating system trust store and ensuring that outbound traffic during execution connects exclusively to official Google domains.",
